{"id":"596d1343-c6c7-4258-856f-c92293f48018","object":"chat.completion","created":1784831687,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case.get('payload')\\n    if not payload:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing payload')\\n    nodes = payload.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = payload.get('required_symbols')\\n    if nodes is None or required_symbols is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing nodes or required_symbols')\\n    if not nodes:\\n        raise ValueError('Empty node list')\\n    node_map = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n.get('id')\\n        if not nid:\\n            raise ValueError('Node missing id')\\n        if 'parent' not in n or 'symbol' not in n or 'depth' not in n or 'removable' not in n:\\n            raise ValueError('Node missing required field')\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(n['id'])\\n    sym_nodes = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        sym_nodes.setdefault(sym, set()).add(n['id'])\\n    all_ids = set(node_map.keys())\\n    initial_present = all_ids\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in sym_nodes or not (sym_nodes[sym] & initial_present):\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym} not present initially')\\n    def subtree_ids(start):\\n        stack = [start]\\n        ids = set()\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur in ids:\\n                continue\\n            ids.add(cur)\\n            for child in children.get(cur, []):\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        return ids\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        if not n.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        if n.get('parent') is None:\\n            continue\\n        sub_ids = subtree_ids(nid)\\n        new_removed = removed | sub_ids\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            present_syms = sym_nodes[sym] - new_removed\\n            if not present_syms:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if ok:\\n            removed.update(sub_ids)\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained = sorted(all_ids - removed)\\n    return {'retained': retained, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the task. The user provides a fixture with several cases. The task is to implement a function `solve(case)` that performs \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The description suggests we are given a tree (nodes with parent, depth, symbol, removable, utility). We need to reduce the tree by removing nodes (tentatively) while preserving required_symbols and grammar validity? The description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\", \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\", but the actual problem seems to be: given a tree with nodes having 'removable' flag, we want to remove nodes in order (deeper first, then shallower, tie-break by node ID) but only if after removal, all required_symbols are still present (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains in the tree). Also we must not remove root, required symbols? Actually root is not removable (removable=false given), but we should never remove root. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Wait: \"a node with retained children\" means if a node has children that we are keeping, we cannot remove it? That would make sense: if you remove a node, you remove its entire subtree. So you can only remove a leaf? Actually in tree reduction, removing a node typically means removing that node and its subtree. But if a node has children that are not removed, you cannot remove the node because it would also remove its children. So we need to consider that we are removing nodes, and removal of a node removes its subtree. So we can only remove a node if either it has no children (leaf) or if we intend to remove all its children as well (but then they would be removed anyway). The typical algorithm: iterate nodes in some order, and for each node that is removable, check if removing it (i.e., deleting that node and its entire subtree) still leaves the required symbols present in the remaining tree. If yes, remove it. The order: deeper first (higher depth), then shallower, tie-break by node ID (stable). Also we must not remove root (given), and not remove required symbols? The required_symbols list likely includes the symbols that must appear at least once. So we must ensure after removal, every symbol in required_symbols still appears in the remaining nodes. Also note that \"removable\" flag indicates whether the node can be considered for removal. Some nodes may be non-removable (like root, or nodes that must stay). So algorithm: Build tree structure from nodes list. Map node ID to node dict. Compute children list. Then determine removal order: sort nodes by (depth DESC, ID). For each node in that order (but only if removable flag is True, and not root? root removable flag is false anyway), check if after removing this node (and its subtree) the required symbols are still present. But also need to check if node has any children that have already been removed? Actually if we remove a node, we also remove its children. But if we are iterating in descending depth order, we will process deeper nodes first. When we consider a node, its descendants may have already been removed earlier if they were eligible. However, removal of a node implies removal of its entire subtree, so if a deeper node was removed, its parent's subtree is already partially removed. But the algorithm typically works by tentatively removing nodes that are currently \"removable\" (i.e., they are still present). After we remove a node, it is removed from consideration. So we should simulate removals: start with all nodes present, then for each node in order, if it is present (not yet removed) and removable, check if required symbols still satisfied after removal. If yes, mark it as removed. Continue. That matches the description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The phrase \"a node with retained children\" suggests that if a node has any child that we intend to keep (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove the node. But in our simulation, if we process deeper first, children may have been removed already. So a node may have some children that are already removed. Then its remaining children are those not yet removed. If it has any remaining children, then removing the node would also remove those children. So we should check if after removing the node, the required symbols are still present. That automatically handles the \"retained children\" issue because if a child has a required symbol, removing it would lose it. But the statement \"a node with retained children\" might be an additional constraint: even if removal would not violate required symbols, we should not remove a node that still has children (i.e., we can only remove leaves or nodes whose entire subtree we are removing). However, if we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree, so it's allowed to have children that are also being removed. But if some children are already removed (i.e., they were processed earlier and removed), then the node's subtree is partially removed. Removing the node would remove the remaining children. That's fine as long as required symbols hold. But the phrase \"a node with retained children\" might mean that we cannot remove a node if it has any child that is not also being removed (i.e., that child is \"retained\"). In our simulation, if a child was not removed earlier, it is retained. So removing the parent would remove it. That's fine if it's allowed. However, the statement \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" suggests that we should not remove a node that still has children (i.e., we can only remove leaves). But that would be contradictory to typical tree reduction where you can remove an internal node and its entire subtree. The Perses algorithm actually removes nodes and replaces them with their children? No, the description says \"deletion and replacement candidates\". The problem might be more complex: there is grammar-guided reduction where you might replace a node with its children, or delete it. But the fixture only has a simple tree with no alternatives. The user says: \"Implement `grammar_guided_node_reduction` through `solve(case)` for this bounded objective: Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" That sounds like we have a tree and we want to remove nodes (delete them) but only if the resulting tree is still \"grammar-valid\" (whatever that means) and maintains \"interestingness\" (which might be the required symbols condition?). Given the simple fixture, perhaps the grammar is trivial: any tree is valid as long as it contains all required symbols. The \"interestingness oracle\" is just the condition that required_symbols are present. The \"deletion and replacement candidates\" might be that we can either delete a node (remove it and its subtree) or replace a node with its children (i.e., replace the node by its children, effectively pruning it but keeping its children). But the fixture only mentions removable flag. \"removable\" might indicate that a node can be removed (deleted) but not replaced. The description also says \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So we are to implement a simplified version: we can delete nodes, and we must decide which ones to delete in order, respecting the constraints.\n\nGiven the limited information, I'll assume the following: The tree is represented by nodes with IDs, parent references, symbols, depth, removable flag, utility (ignored? maybe for ordering? but we use depth and ID). Required symbols must be present in the final tree (i.e., at least one node with that symbol). We can remove nodes that are removable and not root. Removal order: deeper first, tie by ID. We cannot remove a node if it has any children that are not also being removed in the same step? But we are processing one node at a time, so if a node has children, removing it will remove those children. That is allowed as long as the required symbols still hold. So we proceed with the simulation.\n\nThe output should be: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to return two lists: retained_ids (sorted) and removal_order (the order in which nodes were removed). The function should return a JSON-serializable object, so a dict with these keys.\n\nLet's structure the solve function:\n\n- Parse case['payload'] -> nodes list, required_symbols list.\n- Build a dict from node id to node object (including children list).\n- Build a dict from symbol to list of node ids that have that symbol (to quickly check if a symbol is still present).\n- Determine initial set of all node ids. We'll have a set of removed_ids initially empty, and a set of present_ids = all ids.\n- Define a function check_required_symbols(present_ids) that checks for each symbol in required_symbols if there exists at least one node with that symbol in present_ids.\n- Produce a list of nodes sorted by (depth DESC, id ASC). Since depth is given in node, we can use it.\n- Iterate through sorted nodes:\n  - if node id is already removed, skip.\n  - if node['removable'] is False, skip.\n  - if node['parent'] is None: root, skip (though removable false anyway).\n  - Tentatively remove node: compute new_present_ids = present_ids - subtree_ids(node). subtree_ids includes node and all descendants recursively.\n  - After removal, check if all required symbols are still present in new_present_ids. We can compute by checking each symbol: if the only node(s) with that symbol were in the removed subtree, then fail. So we can precompute for each symbol the set of node ids that have that symbol. Then after removal, we check if for each symbol, the set of nodes with that symbol minus removed_ids is non-empty.\n  - If condition holds, then actually remove: add node id and its subtree ids to removed_ids, append node id to removal_order list.\n- After processing all nodes, retained_ids = sorted list of present_ids (those not removed). present_ids = all_ids - removed_ids.\n- Return {\"retained\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nEdge cases: root may have removable False, but ensure we never remove root.\n\nThe fixture shows that root has utility 1.0, but we ignore utility.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) with proper error handling? They said \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if case is missing fields or invalid structure, raise ValueError. We'll do basic validation: ensure 'payload' exists, has 'nodes' list and 'required_symbols' list. Check that nodes have required fields.\n\nWrite Python code accordingly.\n\nNow, produce JSON output with key 'implementation' containing the Python source. Escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nMake sure the function does not mutate case.\n\nAlso, note that the problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort by depth descending, then id ascending. Ensure stable.\n\nImplement subtree_ids function using recursion or iterative. Since tree can be large, but we can compute by BFS/DFS given parent pointers.\n\nWe can precompute children list for each node.\n\nImplementation skeleton:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case.get('payload')\n    if not payload: raise ValueError(\"Missing payload\")\n    nodes = payload.get('nodes')\n    req_syms = payload.get('required_symbols')\n    if nodes is None or req_syms is None: raise ValueError(\"Missing nodes or required_symbols\")\n    # Build node map\n    node_map = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n.get('id')\n        if not nid: raise ValueError\n        node_map[nid] = n\n    # Build children list\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\n    for n in nodes:\n        parent = n.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children[parent].append(n['id'])\n    # Build symbol sets\n    sym_nodes = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        sym_nodes.setdefault(sym, set()).add(n['id'])\n    # Initial present set\n    all_ids = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed = set()\n    removal_order = []\n    # Check initial required symbols\n    for sym in req_syms:\n        if sym not in sym_nodes or len(sym_nodes[sym]) == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {sym} not present initially\")\n    # Helper to get subtree ids\n    def subtree_ids(start):\n        stack = [start]\n        ids = set()\n        while stack:\n            cur = stack.pop()\n            if cur in ids:\n                continue\n            ids.add(cur)\n            for child in children.get(cur, []):\n                stack.append(child)\n        return ids\n    # Order nodes\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        if not n.get('removable', False):\n            continue\n        if n.get('parent') is None: # root\n            continue\n        # Tentative removal\n        sub_ids = subtree_ids(nid)\n        new_removed = removed | sub_ids\n        # Check required symbols\n        ok = True\n        for sym in req_syms:\n            present_syms = sym_nodes[sym] - new_removed\n            if not present_syms:\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if ok:\n            removed.update(sub_ids)\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained = sorted(all_ids - removed)\n    return {\"retained\": retained, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nBut we must ensure that we don't remove nodes that have children that are not removed? Actually we already check required symbols, so it's fine. However, there's a nuance: if we remove a node, we also remove its children, even if those children were not processed yet (i.e., they are deeper). But our iteration order processes deeper first. So suppose we have a node at depth 1 with children at depth 2. In the sorted list, depth 2 nodes appear before depth 1 nodes. So we might remove a depth 2 node first. Then later, when we consider the depth 1 node, its subtree includes the depth 2 nodes, but those have already been removed. The subtree_ids function will include those already removed nodes (since they are descendants). But since they are already removed, removing them again is fine, but the check will be based on new_removed = removed | sub_ids. That will still work. But is it allowed to remove a node after its descendants have been removed? That's typical: if you remove the parent, you are pruning the remaining subtree. It's allowed.\n\nNow, consider the constraint \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" The phrase might mean that you cannot remove a node if it has any child that is not also being removed in the same step (i.e., if you remove a node, you must also remove all its children). But our algorithm removes the entire subtree, which includes all children, so that's satisfied. However, if a child was already removed earlier (because it was a leaf and eligible), then removing the parent later would remove that child again (but it's already gone). That might be considered \"retained children\"? No, because the child is not retained. So it's fine.\n\nThus, the algorithm seems correct.\n\nWe should also ensure that we do not remove a node that has a child with \"removable=False\"? Actually removable flag is per node. If a child is not removable, we still can remove it if we remove the parent? Because removing the parent removes the entire subtree regardless of removable flag. But the problem likely expects that non-removable nodes cannot be removed even as part of a subtree? The description says \"removable\" flag indicates if it can be removed. Usually, if a node is not removable, it cannot be removed directly, but if its parent is removed, it goes away. That might conflict. The Perses algorithm typically only deletes nodes that are \"removable\" (i.e., deletable). Non-removable nodes are structural anchors that must stay. So if a non-removable node exists as a child of a removable node, we cannot remove the parent because that would remove the non-removable child? Possibly. The condition \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" might refer to that: a node cannot be removed if it has children that are not being removed (i.e., children that are retained). But if a child is non-removable, it would be retained, so we cannot remove the parent. Our algorithm would check required symbols, but if the non-removable child has a required symbol, then removal would violate. If it doesn't have a required symbol, is it allowed to remove it? The problem might expect that non-removable nodes should never be removed, even indirectly. The phrase \"a node with retained children\" suggests that if a node has children that are intended to be kept (i.e., not removed), you cannot remove the node. But the algorithm decides which nodes to keep based on removability and other conditions. I think the safest interpretation is: we can only remove nodes that are marked removable, and when we remove a node, we also remove all its descendants regardless of their removability. That's the typical tree pruning: you delete a node, and its subtree disappears. Non-removable nodes are just markers that they themselves cannot be individually removed, but they can be removed as part of a parent deletion if the parent is removable. However, if a non-removable node is a child of a removable node, removing the parent might be disallowed because it would remove the non-removable node, which might be needed. But the problem does not explicitly forbid that. The only constraints are: never remove root, required symbol nodes, or nodes with retained children. \"Nodes with retained children\" -- if we remove a parent, its children are not retained, so it's fine. But what about a child that is non-removable? That child would be removed, so it's not \"retained\". So perhaps it's allowed. To be safe, I'll stick with the simple interpretation: remove removable nodes only, and removal removes subtree, check required symbols.\n\nGiven the fixture, all nodes have removable True except root and some others. No non-removable children of removable parents? Actually in the fixture, n1 (depth1) is not removable, and n1 has child n6 (depth2, removable). So n2 (depth1) is removable and has child n4 (depth2, removable). n3 (depth1) is not removable and has child n5 (depth2, not removable). So there is a non-removable child n5 under non-removable parent n3. That's fine. The algorithm would consider removing n5? n5 is not removable, so skip. Removing n3? n3 is not removable, skip. Removing n2? n2 is removable, and its child n4 is removable, so removing n2 removes n4 as well. That's allowed if required symbols still hold (s1 is needed, there is also n1 with s1). So it's fine.\n\nThus, the algorithm is plausible.\n\nNow, write the final JSON. The response must be a single JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing the Python source. We need to ensure escaping. We'll write the Python code as a multi-line string and then JSON-encode it.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case.get('payload')\\n    if not payload:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing payload')\\n    nodes = payload.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = payload.get('required_symbols')\\n    if nodes is None or required_symbols is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing nodes or required_symbols')\\n    ...\"}\n\nBut note: The response must start with { and end with }. So we produce the JSON directly.\n\nLet's write the full code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1849,"completion_tokens":4933,"total_tokens":6782,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4292},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":57},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}